 re-election in a Montana district that leaned 15 points toward the Republicans at the presidential level. His Democratic challenger, Brian Miller, secured the June nomination but has raised under $20,000 and reported roughly $3,000 on hand, while Downing has raised more than $2.1 million. An independent, Michael Eisenhauer, and a Libertarian are also on the ballot.\n\nA September poll from Rutgers Eagleton showed Downing ahead 48 percent to 26 percent, and prediction markets give Miller only about a 5 percent chance. Downing's approval stood at 34 percent in a May survey, a soft figure that has not made the race competitive. The district has not sent a Democrat to Congress in half a century, and no outside spending has entered on either side.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0084, "margin": -18.82}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0066, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0073, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0079, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0127, "margin": -18.0}]}